['Here is a basic solution in C. It uses a combination of pthreads and time functions to implement the log level functionality as well as prevent redundant logging within a specified time frame. However, this does not handle system dependencies such as file I/O operations or external data sources for log rotation. This would require integration with a system library like `syslog` which can't be done in pure C due to OS limitations.

```C
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char* message;
    time_t timestamp;
} LogEntry;

LogEntry* logBuffer = NULL;
int bufferSize = 0;
pthread_mutex_t lock;
time_t cooldownTime = 60 * 5; // 5 minutes in seconds

void writeToErrorLog(const char* message, int level) {
    if (level < 1 || level > 3) {
        printf("Invalid log level. Choose between 1 to 3.\n");
        return;
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    time_t currentTime = time(NULL);

    for (int i = 0; i < bufferSize; ++i) {
        if ((currentTime - logBuffer[i].timestamp) > cooldownTime && !strcmp(message, logBuffer[i].message)) {
            printf("Redundant log message.\n");
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
            return;
        }
    }

    // Append to the buffer if not a redundant log. If buffer is full, shift all messages up by one position.
    for (int i = bufferSize - 1; i > 0; --i) {
        logBuffer[i] = logBuffer[i-1];
    }

    LogEntry newEntry = { message, currentTime };
    logBuffer[0] = newEntry;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}
```
To handle system dependencies like file I/O operations and external data sources for log rotation, you would need to integrate with a system library or use an existing logging library that provides this functionality. For instance, the `syslog` library in Unix-based systems can be used as follows:

```C
#include <syslog.h>

void writeToErrorLog(const char* message, int level) {
    openlog("my_daemon", LOG_PID | LOG_CONS , LOG_USER);

    switch (level) {
        case 1: syslog(LOG_DEBUG, "%s", message); break;
        case 2: syslog(LOG_INFO, "%s", message); break;
        case 3: syslog(LOG_ERR, "%s", message); break;
        default: printf("Invalid log level. Choose between 1 to 3.\n"); return;
    }

    closelog();
}
```
Please note that the above solution is a basic demonstration and might need further adjustments according to your specific requirements like dynamic buffer size, error handling etc. It also doesn't handle any kind of file rotation or log level strings, you would need to add those based on what fits best for your use case.
']

