['Sure, here's a basic implementation using Java 8's Stream API to handle the weakness lookup and error handling for missing or malformed data. This is not an exhaustive solution but provides a good starting point. It also makes use of Gson library to parse JSON file into objects and uses switch statement to find type strengths.

```java
import java.io.*;
import com.google.gson.Gson;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Paths;

class Pokemon {
    String name;
    String[] types;
}

class TypeStrength {
    String type;
    String[] strengths;
}

public class Main {
    private static final Gson gson = new Gson();
    private static final String CONFIG_PATH = "config.json";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Loading the config file
        String jsonConfig = new String(Files.readAllBytes(Paths.get(CONFIG_PATH)));

        // Parse the JSON into TypeStrength objects
        TypeStrength[] typeStrengths = gson.fromJson(jsonConfig, TypeStrength[].class);

        Pokemon pokemon = new Pokemon();
        pokemon.name = "Pikachu";
        pokemon.types = new String[]{"Electric"};

        System.out.println("Pokemon: " + pokemon.name);
        for (String type : pokemon.types) {
            findStrength(type, typeStrengths).forEach((strength) -> {
                System.out.println("Weakness against " + type + " is " + strength);
            });
        }
    }

    private static List<String> findStrength(String type, TypeStrength[] strengths) {
        return Arrays.stream(strengths)
                .filter((ts) -> ts.type.equalsIgnoreCase(type))
                .findFirst()
                .map(Arrays::asList)
                .orElseGet(Collections::emptyList);
    }
}
```
This example assumes that you have a `config.json` file with the following structure:
```json
[
  {"type": "Fire", "strengths": ["Water"]},
  {"type": "Electric", "strengths": ["Ground"]},
]
```
Please note, this is a basic implementation and there are many other considerations that need to be taken care of in a real-world scenario. It does not handle every edge case mentioned in the problem statement but it serves as a starting point for such an approach. 

You might want to consider more sophisticated error handling (e.g., using exceptions instead of returning empty lists), efficient memory management, and performance optimizations depending on your specific use case.
