["You can use Node.js File System module(fs) to read from the configuration file, then load service names into an array. JavaScript's RegExp constructor can take a string that represents a regular expression, which we will dynamically build using our array of service names and other required parameters for matching whole words in text ignoring punctuation. 

We'll use String.prototype.matchAll to get all matches from the text as an iterable object, then count occurrences with Map to avoid additional pass over the data, improving performance on large inputs. In terms of memory usage, we are not storing each match separately in a massive array, instead using a Map that stores counts directly and only for found words.

Here's a possible implementation:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const encoding = 'utf8'; // adjust to your needs

function loadServices() {
  const servicesData = fs.readFileSync('./services.txt', encoding);
  return servicesData.split(/\r?\n/).map((service) => service.trim());
}

function findCloudTermsCount(text) {
  const services = loadServices();
  const termMap = new Map();

  // Build a regex to match whole words from the service list, ignore punctuation and special characters.
  const termsRegex = new RegExp('\\b(' + services.join('|') + ')\\b', 'gi');
  const matches = text.matchAll(termsRegex);

  // Count occurrences of each match and store in the map.
  for (const [match] of matches) {
    termMap.set(match, (termMap.get(match) || 0) + 1);
  }

  return Object.fromEntries(termMap); // Convert to plain object for easier use
}
```
To handle different formats and character encodings gracefully, you can update the 'encoding' constant as needed when reading files with fs module functions.

This solution is efficient because it minimizes memory usage by only storing counts of found words in a map instead of all matches in an array. It also uses a set-like data structure (Map) for fast lookup and counting, which improves speed even further compared to using arrays or plain objects. However, this trade-off could increase memory usage as we are now storing two pieces of information per word: the name and its count.
"]

['Here is an implementation of the problem as described. This example uses recursive rendering for nested script objects, and conditional rendering based on boolean flags. It also properly escapes strings to prevent injection attacks. 

```javascript
function renderScriptObjects(scriptObject) {
    // Check if script object is defined or not
    if (!scriptObject || typeof scriptObject !== 'object') return;

    for (let key in scriptObject) {
        let value = scriptObject[key];

        // If value is a boolean and it's true, render the element
        if (typeof value === "boolean" && value) {
            document.write(`<${escapeHtml(key)}>`);
            continue;
        }

        // If value is an object or array, recursively render its elements
        if (value && typeof value === 'object') {
            renderScriptObjects(value);
            continue;
        }

        // If value is a string, escape it and append to the element
        if (typeof value === 'string') {
            document.write(`<${escapeHtml(key)}>${escapeHtml(value)}</${escapeHtml(key)}>`);
        }
    }
}

// Function to escape special characters in HTML strings
function escapeHtml(unsafe) {
    return unsafe
         .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
         .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
         .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
         .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
         .replace(/'/g, "&#039;");
}
```

This function uses document.write() to directly modify the HTML of the page it's called on. Please note that this method is not recommended for serious web development due to various reasons including security issues and performance impact. It would be more appropriate to use DOM manipulation methods like `createElement`, `appendChild` etc., or a library/framework like ReactJS which handle these things in a safer way.
']

