            try {
                service.start();
                Thread.sleep(random.nextInt(10000));
                service.stop();
                long stopTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                // Log start and stop times
                // Send stop message to messaging system
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

**Extended Description**:
The program should manage a service's lifecycle in a way that it can be started and stopped repeatedly with random delays. It must ensure that each operation is logged efficiently and that messages are sent reliably to an external messaging system. Throttling must be implemented to prevent excessive resource usage, especially during high-frequency operations. The program should also be able to adapt its behavior based on varying loads and dynamically adjust throttling limits using data from a configuration management system.

response:
['```java
import java.util.*;
import java.time.*;
import org.apache.log4j.Logger;
import com.example.messagingSystemAPI.MessagingSystem;
import com.example.serviceAPI.Service;

public class ServiceManager {
    private final Service service;
    private final Random random = new Random();
    private final MessagingSystem messagingSystem;
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(ServiceManager.class);
    private static final int DELAY_RANGE = 10000;
    private long startTime, stopTime;
    private long throttlingLimitPerMinute = 60L * 1000L / ConfigManager.getInstance().getMaxStartsPerMin();

    public ServiceManager(Service service, MessagingSystem messagingSystem) {
        this.service = service;
        this.messagingSystem = messagingSystem;
    }

    public void manageService() {
        while (true) {
            try {
                startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                if(throttleStart()) {
                    logger.info("Service started at " + new Date());
                    service.start();
                    Thread.sleep(random.nextInt(DELAY_RANGE));
                    service.stop();
                    stopTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
                    // Send stop message to messaging system
                    sendStopMessage();
                } else {
                    logger.info("Throttling limit reached, sleeping for " + DELAY_RANGE/10);
                    Thread.sleep(DELAY_RANGE/10);
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
            }
        }
    }

    private boolean throttleStart() {
        long currentTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        if(stopTime - startTime < throttlingLimitPerMinute) {
            return false;
        } else {
            throttlingLimitPerMinute = 60L * 1000L / ConfigManager.getInstance().getMaxStartsPerMin();
            return true;
        }
    }

    private void sendStopMessage() {
        try {
            messagingSystem.send(new Message("Service stopped at " + new Date()));
            logger.info("Sent stop message to messaging system");
        } catch (MessagingException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to send stop message", e);
        }
    }
}
```

This Java program manages a service's lifecycle by repeatedly starting and stopping it with random delays, logging the start and stop times and sending a message to an external messaging system when the service is stopped. The throttling limit for starts per minute is dynamically adjusted based on current system conditions retrieved from a configuration management system. 

Please note that this code uses log4j for logging and assumes existence of the appropriate MessagingSystem and Service classes, along with their associated APIs. Please replace `com.example.messagingSystemAPI.MessagingSystem` and `com.example.serviceAPI.Service` with actual API references of your messaging system and service respectively.
']

