['Here is the PHP function that authenticates a user using OAuth 2.0 client, stores the access token in a session variable and retrieves user information using access token:

```php
function authenticateUser($clientId, $clientSecret) {
    try {
        $oauthClient = new OAuthClient();

        // Set up the parameters for the authentication request
        $params = [
            'grant_type' => 'password',
            'client_id' => $clientId,
            'client_secret' => $clientSecret,
            'username' => $_POST['username'],
            'password' => $_POST['password']
        ];

        $response = $oauthClient->request($params);

        // Store the access token in a session variable
        $_SESSION['access_token'] = $response['access_token'];

        // Retrieve user information using the access token
        $userInfoResponse = $oauthClient->getUserInformation($_SESSION['access_token']);

        return $userInfoResponse;
    } catch (OAuthException $e) {
        // Handle OAuth errors gracefully and implement a fallback mechanism for failed authentications
        $fallback = new FallbackAuthenticator();
        return $fallback->authenticateUser($_POST['username'], $_POST['password']);
    } catch (Exception $e) {
        // Handle any other exceptions gracefully
        error_log("An error occurred: " . $e->getMessage());
        throw new RuntimeException("Failed to authenticate user");
    }
}
```

Here is the JavaScript function that collects data from a network stream and saves it to an array, with pause/resume functionality:

```javascript
let dataStream = []; // This will hold our data stream
let buffer = []; // This will hold data during pauses for buffering
let paused = false; // This is used to check if we are currently paused
let intervalId = null; // Used to clear the interval when needed

function startDataCollection(stream) {
    // Clear any existing interval before starting a new one
    clearInterval(intervalId); 

    intervalId = setInterval(() => {
        if (!paused) {
            let dataChunk = stream.read();

            // If the chunk of data is not empty, add it to our buffer
            if (dataChunk) {
                buffer.push(dataChunk);
            } else {
                // If the chunk of data was empty, we have finished a pause and can save our buffered data
                paused = true;

                // Add the buffered data to our main data stream and clear the buffer for future pauses
                dataStream.push(...buffer);
                buffer = [];
            }
        } else {
            // If we are currently paused, check if there is new data in the buffer and save it if necessary
            let newDataChunk = stream.read();

            if (newDataChunk) {
                paused = false;

                // Save any remaining buffered data
                if (buffer.length > 0) {
                    dataStream.push(...buffer);
                    buffer = [];
                }

                // Add the new data to our main data stream
                dataStream.push(newDataChunk);
            }
        }
    }, 1000); // Adjust this interval as needed to meet your needs
}
```
This code is a general guide and will need adjustments based on specific use cases, the structure of the OAuth client you're using, the specifics of how data is sent from the IoT device, and your database schema for storing user information and stream data.
