['Below is a simplified example of how you can implement such functionality using C# with LRU (Least Recently Used) cache and MD5 hashing. Note that this is just a basic implementation. The actual solution would require additional considerations, such as proper exception handling, logging, testing etc.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory;

public class HashService
{
    private readonly MemoryCache _cache = new MemoryCache(new MemoryCacheOptions()
    {
        SizeLimit = 1000 // Maximum size limit of the cache is 1000 entries
    });

    public string GetMD5Hash(string input)
    {
        if (!_cache.TryGetValue(input, out string cachedResult))
        {
            using (var md5 = MD5.Create())
            {
                byte[] data = md5.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(input));
                StringBuilder sBuilder = new StringBuilder();

                for (int i = 0; i < data.Length; i++)
                    sBuilder.Append(data[i].ToString("x2"));

                cachedResult = sBuilder.ToString(); 
            }

            var cacheEntryOptions = new MemoryCacheEntryOptions()
                .SetSize(1) // Assume each cache entry is 1 as per the problem requirement. You can tweak this based on actual data size and number of entries.
                .SetSlidingExpiration(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2)); // Slide expiration after every two minutes to keep cache fresh.

            _cache.Set(input, cachedResult, cacheEntryOptions);
        }

        return cachedResult;
    }
}
```
The above example uses in-memory caching using `MemoryCache` from `Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Memory` namespace. It stores the hashes in an LRU (Least Recently Used) cache to ensure that the least recently accessed items are evicted when space runs out.

For a distributed, scalable solution with concurrent requests and high availability you would need to integrate it with a Redis-based caching system or similar technology. This is beyond the scope of this simple example but can be done by utilizing libraries such as `StackExchange.Redis` for .NET, which supports multiple concurrency models (async/await, synchronous) and includes connection pooling and failover support.

