    return promiseChain.then(async () => {
        try {
            // Use node-fetch to call external API for metadata about the files
            const response = await fetch('http://externalapi/metadata');
            const data = await response.json();
            if (data && !data.skip) {
                execFile('node', [currentScript], (error, stdout, stderr) => {
                    if (error) {
                        console.warn(`exec error: ${error}`);
                    }
                    // Log the output of the script to the console
                    console.log(stdout); 
                });
            } else {
                console.log('Skipping file as per metadata');
            }
        } catch (e) {
            console.error('Error occurred while executing:', e);
        }
    });
}, Promise.resolve());
```

In this JavaScript code, we first load the 'node-fetch' library to call external APIs and use it to get metadata about the files. We then sequentially execute each file using the `execFile` function from the 'child_process' module, which allows us to run a new Node.js process for each script. The scripts are executed asynchronously, meaning that one does not have to finish before the next starts.
