['The following code block implements a basic version of the described Java function using Spring framework. It includes some simplifications due to the complexity of the problem domain and the limitations of this platform:

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.web.socket.TextMessage;
import org.springframework.web.socket.WebSocketSession;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class WebSocketService {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(WebSocketService.class);

    // Configure this value as per your needs
    private final int MAX_QUEUE_SIZE = 100;

    // Configure this value as per your needs
    private final long TIMEOUT = 2000L;

    private BlockingQueue<Message> messageQueue = new LinkedBlockingDeque<>(MAX_QUEUE_SIZE);

    private ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());

    private Map<String, WebSocketSession> sessionMap; // You might want to replace this with your own map implementation.

    public void handleMessage(String userId, String courseId, String messageContent) {
        if (userId == null || courseId == null || messageContent == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid parameters");
        }

        Message message = new Message(userId, courseId, messageContent);
        try {
            boolean offered = messageQueue.offer(message, TIMEOUT, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);

            if (!offered) {
                throw new IllegalStateException("Message queue is full");
            }

            executorService.submit(() -> sendAndLog(message));
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    private void sendAndLog(Message message) {
        WebSocketSession session = sessionMap.get(message.courseId);
        if (session == null) {
            logger.warn("No active sessions for course: {}", message.courseId);
            return;
        }

        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            session.sendMessage(new TextMessage(message.content));

            logger.info("Sent message to user: {}, course: {}, time taken: {} ms", 
                    message.userId, message.courseId, System.currentTimeMillis() - startTime);
        } catch (IOException | RuntimeException e) {
            logger.error("Failed to send message to user: {}, course: {}, exception: ", 
                    message.userId, message.courseId, e);
        }
    }

    private static class Message {
        final String userId;
        final String courseId;
        final String content;

        public Message(String userId, String courseId, String content) {
            this.userId = userId;
            this.courseId = courseId;
            this.content = content;
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a `BlockingQueue` for the message queue and an executor service to handle concurrent tasks. You might want to replace this with your own map implementation for storing web socket sessions. 

The function `sendAndLog()` logs the time taken to send each message, which can be used for performance monitoring. It also handles exceptions that may occur when sending a message and logs them. Note that it assumes that the WebSocketSession already contains all necessary information about the connection (e.g., hostname or token) so that it can send messages without needing external API calls.

Please make sure to adapt this code to your own needs, especially regarding data validation, error handling, and performance metrics. Also, keep in mind that the actual implementation will depend on your specific requirements, such as the version of Java you are using, the Spring version, your database setup, etc.
']

