['Here's some basic implementation of your requirements. Since you didn't mention any particular programming language or technology stack, I chose to use Java with Spring Boot and AspectJ for aspect-oriented programming which could be useful in implementing logging, error handling, configuring parameters from a file etc. This is quite complex task so it would take significant amount of time to fully implement all the requirements but here's the starting point:

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.net.*;
import org.aspectj.lang.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.stereotype.*;
import org.apache.commons.configuration2.*;
import org.slf4j.*;
import org.apache.commons.io.*;
import ch.qos.logback.classic.spi.*;

@Service
public class FileStorageManager {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(FileStorageManager.class);

    @GuardBy("this")
    private ExecutorService executor;

    @GuardBy("this")
    private int currentUploadCount = 0;

    @GuardBy("this")
    private List<Future<?>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

    public FileStorageManager(int maxConcurrentUploads) {
        executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(maxConcurrentUploads);
    }

    @Synchronized
    public void uploadFile(Path path) throws Exception {
        while (true) {
            if (currentUploadCount < max_concurrent_uploads) {
                currentUploadCount++;
                break;
            } else {
                Thread.sleep(50); // backoff strategy, adjust as needed
            }
        }

        Future<?> future = executor.submit(() -> {
            try {
                byte[] data = Files.readAllBytes(path);

                if (data.length > max_data_size) {
                    throw new IOException("File size exceeded limit");
                }

                // process file data...

            } catch (Exception e) {
                logger.error("Failed to upload file", e);
            } finally {
                synchronized(this) { currentUploadCount--; }
            }
        });

        futures.add(future);
    }

    @Synchronized
    public void waitForCompletion() throws Exception {
        for (Future<?> future : futures) {
            future.get(); // blocks until task is done or an exception occurs
        }

        futures.clear();
    }
}
```

To manage the configuration file and network connectivity, Spring Boot provides a very convenient way with `@ConfigurationProperties`. Similarly, for logging rotation and archiving, you could use Logback's `SizeAndTimeBasedRollingPolicy` or a custom solution using `ch.qos.logback.core.rolling.RollingFileAppender`.

Please note that this is a basic implementation and does not cover all the aspects mentioned in your requirements, such as exponential backoff, thread safety for memory allocation, error handling, log rotation with compression etc. Each of these topics requires substantial code which would make this post way too long. The key point here is to show you how to start thinking about implementing this task in Java using modern and robust frameworks and libraries.
