["Here's a basic implementation of the task list in C using pthread library for thread management. In this version, memory allocation errors are handled by returning NULL pointers from functions that may fail. Memory is allocated with `malloc`, and all data structures are protected with locks to provide thread safety. 

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct task {
    char* data;
    int priority;
    struct task* next;
} task;

task** initialize_tasks(int num_tasks, int min_priority, int max_priority, int max_data_length) {
    pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
    srand(time(NULL)); // initialize random seed

    task** head = malloc(sizeof(task*));
    if (head == NULL) return NULL;

    for (int i = 0; i < num_tasks; i++) {
        pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); // lock critical section

        task* newTask = malloc(sizeof(task));
        if (newTask == NULL) return NULL;

        int priority = rand() % (max_priority - min_priority + 1) + min_priority; // random priority in range [min_priority, max_priority]
        char* data = malloc(sizeof(char) * (rand() % max_data_length + 1)); // random length string
        if (data == NULL) return NULL;

        for (int j = 0; j < strlen(data); j++) {
            data[j] = 'a' + rand() % 26; // fill with random lowercase letters
        }

        newTask->priority = priority;
        newTask->data = data;
        newTask->next = NULL;

        task** temp = head;
        while (*temp != NULL) {
            if ((*temp)->priority < priority) break; // insert in ascending order of priority
            temp = &((*temp)->next);
        }
        newTask->next = *temp;
        *temp = newTask;

        pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); // unlock critical section
    }

    return head;
}
``` 

This implementation uses a simple linked list data structure. When adding tasks, it inserts them into the list in ascending order of priority to maintain order as specified by the task. It also ensures thread safety with mutexes when modifying the linked list to avoid race conditions. However, this may have performance issues if there are many tasks and contention for the lock becomes a bottleneck; an alternative approach could be using reader-writer locks for non-conflicting access but more complex synchronization primitives for conflicting access (which can degrade performance).
"]

