['Here's a sample implementation in C and Java for the problem statement. Please note that error handling, logging, concurrency management, and signal handling are not covered here. You need to add those parts according to your specific requirements.

**C Code**

The fork() system call is used to create a new process (a child process), which starts running concurrently with the calling process (the parent). The waitpid() system call suspends execution of its calling process until one of its children terminates. The return status from waitpid can be examined to see if the child exited normally and what its return value was.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <signal.h>

void handle_signal(int signum) {
    // Log signal interruptions
    printf("Caught signal %d\n", signum);
}

int runCommand(char *cmd) {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid < 0) {
        perror("fork");
        return 0;
    }

    if (pid == 0) {
        // Child process
        execlp(cmd, cmd, NULL);
        perror("execlp");
        exit(1);
    } else {
        struct sigaction sa;
        sa.sa_handler = handle_signal;
        sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
        sa.sa_flags = 0;

        // Set signal handler for child process
        if (sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL) == -1) {
            perror("sigaction");
            return 0;
        }

        int status;
        waitpid(pid, &status, 0);

        // Check child process's exit status
        if (!WIFEXITED(status) || WEXITSTATUS(status) != 0) {
            printf("Child exited abnormally\n");
            return 0;
        }
    }

    return 1;
}
```
**Java Code**

The Java ObjectInputStream class is used to read primitive data types and strings, as well as objects. This class extends the DataInputStream class, which provides methods for reading bytes from a binary stream. The method reads the bytes of serialized object and convert them into object.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class JavaClass {
    public static Object deserializeObject(byte[] data) throws RuntimeException {
        if (data == null || data.length == 0) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Input data is null or empty");
        }

        try (ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(data);
             ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bis)) {

            return ois.readObject();
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to deserialize object", e);
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simple implementation and doesn't cover all the requirements mentioned in this task. You may need additional error handling, logging mechanisms, concurrency management, etc. 

Also, remember that serialized objects can be manipulated to execute arbitrary code when deserialized, thus security considerations should also be taken into account for a production environment.
