For the second part of your task, which involves creating a new directory with a given name and setting an environment variable to its path using Rust. The function checks if an environment variable is already set with the path to an existing directory and use that path if it exists. Otherwise, it creates a new directory and sets the environment variable to the path of the new directory.

```rust
use std::env;
use std::fs::{self, File};
use std::io::Write;
use std::path::PathBuf;

pub fn create_directory(name: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {
    let env_var = format_env_var_key(&name);

    if let Ok(Some(dir)) = env::var_os(&env_var) {
        return Some(PathBuf::from(dir));
    }

    let dir_path = create_new_directory(name)?;
    set_environment_variable(&env_var, &dir_path);

    Some(dir_path)
}

fn format_env_var_key(name: &str) -> String {
    format![ENV_VAR_PREFIX "{}", name.to_uppercase()]
}

fn create_new_directory(name: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {
    let dir_path = PathBuf::from(&*env::current_dir().unwrap()).join(name);

    if fs::create_dir(&dir_path).is_err() {
        return None;
    }

    Some(dir_path)
}

fn set_environment_variable(key: &str, value: &PathBuf) -> bool {
    let path = format![r#""{}""#, value.to_string_lossy()];
    env::set_var(key, path).is_ok()
}
```

This Rust code uses Cargo's `env` crate to handle environment variables and the `std::fs` module for file system operations. It creates a new directory using the `create_dir` function of the `std::fs` module, and sets an environment variable using the `set_var` function from the `env` crate. Note that this is just a simple example, you might need to add error handling depending on your specific use case.
']

['Here's a basic skeleton of how you might create the function `initialize_records` in C language. It uses POSIX threads for thread safety and pthread mutex locks to protect shared data from simultaneous access. For simplicity, I am not including parts like external dependencies or logging mechanism. Please adapt according to your needs.

