['Here is the solution in C programming language. This code will create three arrays dynamically and initialize them with random values within a specified range. The function uses pthreads to make it thread-safe, and memory allocation error handling is performed using assertions. 

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <assert.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>

typedef struct {
    int x;
    int y;
} Point;

typedef struct {
    Point point1;
    Point point2;
} Rectangle;

typedef struct {
    double* arrDouble;
    Point* arrPoint;
    Rectangle* arrRectangle;
    int size;
    pthread_mutex_t mutex;
} SharedState;

SharedState sharedState;

void* initializeArrays(void* arg) {
    srand(time(NULL));  // initialize random seed

    int size = *((int*)arg);

    pthread_mutex_lock(&sharedState.mutex);

    double* arrDouble = malloc(sizeof(double)*size);
    assert(arrDouble != NULL && "Memory allocation failed for arrDouble");

    Point* arrPoint = malloc(sizeof(Point)*size);
    assert(arrPoint != NULL && "Memory allocation failed for arrPoint");

    Rectangle* arrRectangle = malloc(sizeof(Rectangle)*size);
    assert(arrRectangle != NULL && "Memory allocation failed for arrRectangle");

    for (int i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        arrDouble[i] = (double)rand() / RAND_MAX; // random double in range [0, 1)
        arrPoint[i].x = rand(); // random int
        arrPoint[i].y = rand(); // random int
        arrRectangle[i].point1.x = rand(); // random int
        arrRectangle[i].point1.y = rand(); // random int
        arrRectangle[i].point2.x = rand(); // random int
        arrRectangle[i].point2.y = rand(); // random int
    }

    sharedState.arrDouble = arrDouble;
    sharedState.arrPoint = arrPoint;
    sharedState.arrRectangle = arrRectangle;
    sharedState.size = size;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sharedState.mutex);

    return NULL;
}
``` 
The `initializeArrays` function is thread-safe because it locks the mutex before modifying the shared state, ensuring that no other threads can access or modify the data while we are updating it. It also checks for memory allocation errors and terminates the program if any allocation fails to help prevent further damage due to failures. 

Remember to use `pthread_mutex_destroy(&sharedState.mutex)` when you're done with the mutex, and `free()` the dynamically allocated arrays when they are no longer needed. 

To run this function in a new thread, call:
```c
int main() {
    pthread_t threadId;
    int size = 1000; // or any other value you want for array size
    if (pthread_create(&threadId, NULL, initializeArrays, &size) != 0) {
        printf("Failed to create the thread\n");
        return 1;
    }

    pthread_join(threadId, NULL); // wait for the thread to finish
}
```
Please note that this is a basic example and can be adapted according to your specific needs. You might need additional functionality or error handling depending on how you want to use it in your application. Also, please remember to handle the memory clean up properly as per your requirements.
