Here's the requested Java function. It uses a Union-Find data structure to count disjoint sets.

```java
import java.util.*;
class Main{
static int find(int parent[], int i) {
    if (parent[i] == -1)
        return i;
    return find(parent, parent[i]);
}

static void Union(int parent[], int x, int y) {
    int xset = find(parent, x);
    int yset = find(parent, y);

    if(xset != yset) 
        parent[xset] = yset;
}

static int countSets(int parent[], int n) {
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
         if (parent[i] == -1)  // If root node, increment count.
             count++;
    }  
    return count;
}

static int disjointPairs(int arr[]) {
     int n = arr.length;

     int parent[] = new int[n];
     Arrays.fill(parent,-1); // Initialize all parents as -1.

     for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
         for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {  // For every pair of elements.
             if (arr[i] == arr[j]) 
                 Union(parent, i , j);
         }   
     }

     return countSets(parent,n);
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
     int arr[] = {1,2,3,4};
     System.out.println("Number of disjoint pairs: " + disjointPairs(arr));
    } 
}
```
Above Java code counts number of distinct pairs in an array where elements are unique and the pairs can be formed by any two distinct element from the array. The Union-Find data structure is used to track whether two numbers are part of the same set or not. The `find` function returns root of a node, while the `Union` function unites sets containing i and j into one. Finally, we count number of roots in parent list which gives us number of disjoint pairs.
']

